{{/* ---------------- /security ---------------- */}}
{{define "security"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>Security · Counterfoil</title><meta name="theme-color" content="#014693">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/app.css"></head>
<body class="aboutpage">
{{template "pubtop" .}}
<section class="ab-band">
  <div class="ab-wrap">
    <h1 class="pubh1">Security<span class="enddot"></span></h1>
    <p class="pubsub">What actually guards this prototype — each item is in the code, most leave receipts,
    and several exist because an automated security review of our own commits found the gap and we fixed it.</p>

    <h2>The gate, before anything leaves</h2>
    <ul class="ab-drills">
      <li><b>Hash-bound mandates.</b> A reserved action dispatches only under a named person's mandate bound
        to the canonical hash of the exact request. Change one rupee after approval and the gate refuses,
        with a receipt. Mandates are single-use and expire in 45 minutes.</li>
      <li><b>Separation of duties.</b> Whoever opened a case cannot decide its reserved approvals, and
        onboarding intake is a different role from the offer's approver by construction.</li>
      <li><b>Scope by role.</b> A manager sees their own reports — on People, profiles, clocks and every
        search result — never the wider workforce or its salaries. Sealed cases stay out of their view.</li>
      <li><b>Fail closed.</b> The duplicate-hire check treats an unreachable directory as UNKNOWN and routes
        to a person; absence counts only when the target itself answers 404. A lying "201 Created" can never
        become Done — read-back decides, and a contradiction goes to a named person.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The record and its signer</h2>
    <ul class="ab-drills">
      <li><b>Hash-chained receipts</b>, countersigned by a separate process that alone holds the signing key.
        The signer remembers what it signed: it refuses to countersign a different history for a sequence it
        already covered, and refuses to go backwards.</li>
      <li><b>Out-of-band pinning.</b> The offline verifier trusts only a signer key delivered outside the
        bundle; a key found inside the bundle is never a pin, and the report says UNPINNED in so many words.</li>
      <li><b>Loopback by default.</b> All three demo processes bind to 127.0.0.1; the signing endpoint takes a
        shared token (constant-time compared) for any shared environment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The application surface</h2>
    <ul class="ab-drills">
      <li><b>The model is confined to typed tools.</b> It cannot name a URL, decide an approval or move money;
        its arguments are validated against declared schemas, its failures are shown as failures, and every
        call is metered on the chain. The API key lives server-side in the environment and appears in no URL,
        log, template, receipt or repository.</li>
      <li><b>Boring web hygiene, verified.</b> Redirect targets are parsed and constrained; the 404 escapes
        what it reflects; PIN re-entry guards decisions; sessions are random ids in HttpOnly cookies.</li>
      <li><b>Drills, not claims.</b> Seven canary probes re-test the guards on demand — forged mandate,
        tampered amount, agent deciding, expired mandate, duplicate intent, lying reply, kill-switch bypass —
        and every run leaves a receipt an auditor can re-check offline.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Identity, deliberately without passwords</h2>
    <ul class="ab-drills">
      <li><b>No self-registration, by design.</b> An internal HR platform is not a consumer site: accounts
        are provisioned by HR and IT through the onboarding journey (a warranted, receipted process), and
        identity belongs to the corporate directory. A register page here would be an attack surface
        pretending to be a feature.</li>
      <li><b>No passwords stored, ever.</b> The demo signs in with a persona picker standing in for
        corporate single sign-on; production is OIDC against the company's identity provider, with
        re-authentication at each reserved decision carrying the mandate's hash as its nonce. The PIN you
        type at an approval is the demo stand-in for exactly that re-login.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This is a hackathon prototype: the demo identity provider is a persona picker with PINs,
    and the stand-in target systems are ours. The production design swaps in OIDC re-authentication and real
    targets without moving any of the guarantees above.</p>
  </div>
</section>
{{template "pubfoot" .}}
</body></html>
{{end}}

{{/* ---------------- /privacy ---------------- */}}
{{define "privacy"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>Privacy · Counterfoil</title><meta name="theme-color" content="#014693">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/static/app.css"></head>
<body class="aboutpage">
{{template "pubtop" .}}
<section class="ab-band">
  <div class="ab-wrap">
    <h1 class="pubh1">Privacy<span class="enddot"></span></h1>
    <p class="pubsub">Not a policy document — a description of how this prototype actually treats personal
    text, enforced by its architecture rather than promised by a page.</p>

    <h2>Personal text never enters the receipts</h2>
    <ul class="ab-drills">
      <li><b>Chat</b> lives in its own store; the exportable chain carries events, never what anyone typed.</li>
      <li><b>Survey answers</b> live in a survey store; the receipt carries the field names and a canonical
        hash that commits to them — enough to prove a submission, nothing to read.</li>
      <li><b>Task notes</b> are hashed into their receipts the same way.</li>
      <li><b>POSH and grievance disclosures</b> are routed to the named officer and sealed: the text reaches
        no model and no receipt; a hash stands in, and the platform never decides these (a reserved rule).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the model sees</h2>
    <p>The assistant reads only what its typed tools return about <i>your own</i> case — the rule pack, your
    steps and clocks, your settlement lines. Staff investigation drafts read a target system's record for one
    item. Sealed text is out of reach by construction, and every model call is metered on the chain with its
    purpose.</p>

    <h2>What leaves this machine</h2>
    <p>In the demo: model calls to the configured AI provider (server-side key, request headers only), and
    nothing else — fonts, styles and scripts are self-hosted; there is no CDN, no analytics, no tracking of
    any kind. Every person and record in the demo is synthetic.</p>

    <h2>Who sees what</h2>
    <p>A leaver sees their own case and its counterfoils. A manager sees their reports. HR operations,
    payroll, administration and audit see the slices their role entitles them to — and the audit trail of
    who decided what is itself part of the record.</p>
  </div>
</section>
{{template "pubfoot" .}}
</body></html>
{{end}}

